2. d3 { D3 - Fisher's choice, part of the Kings Indian attack setup. } 2... d5
3. Nd2 Nf6 4. g3 c5 5. Bg2 Nc6 6. Ngf3 Be7 7. O-O
{ We have the typical setup of the Kings Indian attack for White and the Queens Gambit Declined for black. }
7... O-O
{ We have the typical setup of the Kings Indian attack for White and the Queens Gambit Declined for black. }
8. e5
{ E5 by Fisher - the rook and knight will defend the pawn on E5 and then what follows is pretty straightforward. }
8... Nd7 9. Re1
{ The rook and the knight are defending the pawn on E5. The knight comes to F1 to free the path for the bishop to go to F4 from where it also defends E5. Then H4 is played, hopefully pushing all the way to H6 to weaken dark squares around the black king. }
9... b5
{ B5 - normal play for black, black will seek counterplay on the queen side. }
10. Nf1 { Knight F1 - standard move, part of the king's Indian attack plan. }
10... b4 11. h4
{ H4 - standard move. All these moves can be played in different move orders but it doesn't really affect much as each side is playing on their own wing. }
11... a5 12. Bf4 { Bishop F4 - standard move defending the pawn on E5. } 12...
a4
{ A4 - now we come to the critical moment. If left on their own devices, black will play A3 with the idea to dislodge the pawn from B2, whether by white capturing on A3 or more likely B3 closing the queen side. However after B3, black has the maneuver Knight A7, Knight B5 targeting C3 and D4 squares which will be rather weak after B3. }
13. a3
{ A3 by Fisher himself! He prefers to open the B file but not weaken the dark squares. This is the key idea - playing A3 proactively to stop Black's A3 counterplay. }
13... bxa3 14. bxa3 Na5 15. Ne3 Ba6 16. Bh3
{ Bishop H3 - white is targeting these pawns. Maybe at some point there may be a sacrifice, for example if the queen leaves the defense of the Knight on D7, Knight D5 can happen and if exD5 then the Knight on D7 is hanging. }
16... d4 { D4 attacking the Knight on E3. } 17. Nf1
{ Knight F1 - it was possible to put the Knight on G4 but Fisher wants to keep the D1-H5 diagonal open for the queen. He wants to go Knight G5 and then the queen can come to H5 targeting the H7 pawn. }
17... Nb6
{ Knight B6 - black could have done better, for example Bishop B7 and C4 were better options here. }
18. Ng5
{ Knight G5 - following the idea of an attack. The idea is to go Queen H5. The Knight on G5 and the bishop on H3 are targeting the pawn on A6. The sacrifice may not work but it's still a threat. }
18... Nd5 { Knight D5 centralizing the Knight targeting the bishop. } 19. Bd2
{ Bishop drops back to D2 covering C3 and keeping an eye on the Knight on A5. }
19... Bxg5
{ Black decides to eliminate the Knight because after Queen H5 he would have been forced to do so in view of the threat against the pawn on H7 with mate. }
20. Bxg5 Qd7 21. Qh5 { Queen H5 - getting the queen closer to the king side. }
21... Rfc8 22. Nd2
{ Knight D2 - a very common theme in the King's Indian attack. This knight on F1 can stay there for a while but at a certain point will get activated. Fisher prefers D2 and then E4 because from E4 it targets F6 like from G4 but also targets the D6 pawn - more flexible. }
22... Nc3
{ Knight C3 - stopping Knight E4. But now the knight is no longer controlling the F6 square, which allows Fisher to start a very beautiful attack. }
23. Bf6
{ Bishop F6 - very often in the King's Indian attack white is aiming for the weakening of the dark squares around the black king. }
23... gxf6
{ G takes F6 - E takes F6 was just fatal because the king is too weak, there will be some mating threats. Otherwise the idea is to go Queen G5 and basically mate on G7. }
